<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/strategery-framed-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Screenshot of a tablet game showing a colorful segmented map on dark background." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption>A new, but familiar, Strategery.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Strategery was always one of our very favorite games in the early days of iOS. And by “we,” I mean large portions of the staff at Macworld, including both myself and Lex Friedman. Lex reviewed the app for Macworld, and we even gave it an award. It was a Hall of Fame iPhone game.</p>
<p>Strategery—named for Will Ferrell’s made-up word during his impression of George W. Bush—was a take on the classic strategy board game Risk. Risk is a clunky game to play, with an awful lot of little plastic pieces and dice rolling. Strategery did away with that, resolving battles with a single tap while replacing the map-inspired board with a series of procedurally generated game terrains. It was really great.</p>
<p>Now the bad news: The classic Strategery game, which hasn’t been updated for nearly a decade, doesn’t work on iOS 27, which (if you hadn’t noticed) <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/09/ios-27-review-little-things-mean-a-lot/">came out today</a>. I hate it <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2021/02/the-mortality-of-software/">when beloved software dies</a>. And so does Lex, which is why he sprang into action and began building a game that could be a suitable Strategery replacement. (It was very good! I have played it a bunch over the last few weeks, via TestFlight.)</p>
<p>But then <a href="https://lexontech.org/strategery-is-back-and-im-the-developer">something delightful happened, as Lex recounts</a>:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  I loved Strategery. I didn’t feel good about knocking it off. So I reached out to the developers…. I had several delightful video conversations with Sumul and Courtland. And I lucked out: I found two of the nicest developers you could ever hope to meet. They still felt a ton of love for Strategery, they loved how many people still felt warmly about the game — but they didn’t have time to devote to keeping it updated. We worked out a deal for me to take it on.
</p></blockquote>
<p>The result is that Lex’s game inspired by Strategery has <em>become</em> Strategery! Existing Strategery users will just get it as a free update. And new players can <a href="https://strategerygame.com/app">buy it for $5</a>.</p>
<p>Lex has added a few new features to Strategery, and with the consultation of the original developers, he’s restored some features he hadn’t even realized that he had missed.</p>
<p>What a happy ending. Strategery would always have a place in App Valhalla, but it’s even better if it remains alive and well. Thanks, Lex.</p>
